note right of CallManager
  Mesh initiation rule (issue #46): the newly
  joining peer always offers to everyone
  already in call_roster; existing participants
  only ever answer, in response to an incoming
  offer via callSignalReceived() — never on
  callPeerJoined(), which is purely informational.

  PeerConnectionObserver / SetLocal-/
  SetRemoteDescriptionObserverInterface
  callbacks fire on WebRTC's own signaling
  thread; every one hops back to this class's
  own (GUI) thread via QMetaObject::invokeMethod
  before touching any shared state.

  Real microphone/speaker audio (issue #70):
  joinCall() wires AudioInputDevice::pcmDataAvailable()
  into pushCapturedAudio(), and CallAudioDeviceModule's
  PlayoutSink into AudioOutputDevice::writeAudio() —
  both single-owner-at-a-time with the Settings
  dialog's mic-test/test-tone controls, so a call and
  those controls silently take over the same
  QAudioSource/QAudioSink from each other (accepted
  first-pass tradeoff). A null/unselected QAudioDevice
  is handled with callError() rather than being passed
  through — WebRTC's own AudioTransportImpl hard-aborts
  (fatal CHECK) on a 0 channel count instead of failing
  gracefully.

  Camera video (issue #72): the camera video track is
  created once, on the first enableVideo() call, and
  attached to every existing PeerConnection right there
  — negotiateLocal() is called explicitly for each one,
  the same way ensurePeerConnection()/
  onCallRosterReceived() already negotiate the initial
  audio track, rather than reacting to WebRTC's own
  OnRenegotiationNeeded() notification (deliberately left
  as its inherited no-op). Relying on both an explicit
  call AND that notification for the same track change
  raced them against each other during live testing —
  the queued OnRenegotiationNeeded callback fired again
  after the explicit call had already completed the
  negotiation, stacking a second unwanted offer on top
  and corrupting the exchange ("Called in wrong state:
  stable"). A PeerConnection created after video is
  already on picks the track up as part of its own
  initial offer/answer instead (attachCameraTrack(), also
  called from ensurePeerConnection()). Once attached, the
  track is never removed again — enableVideo()/
  disableVideo() only toggle
  VideoTrackInterface::set_enabled(), the same mechanism
  setMuted() uses for audio — because RemoveTrackOrError()
  hit a real fatal assertion inside WebRTC's own
  codec-list handling during live testing
  (media/base/codec_list.cc, "Check failed: present_codec
  == codec").

  Screen share (issue #112) originally reused camera
  video's exact track/attach machinery instead of a second
  track, mutually exclusive with it — issue #185 split
  that into two fully independent tracks/sources
  (cameraTrackSource_/localCameraTrack_ vs
  screenShareTrackSource_/localScreenShareTrack_), each
  with its own ensureLocalXxxTrack()/attachXxxTrack(),
  sharing the AddTrack()+error-handling body via the small
  attachTrack() helper. enableVideo() and
  enableScreenShare() no longer touch each other — both
  can be on at once, at the cost of a second track's
  renegotiation/attach bookkeeping per peer the first time
  each is used, a cost issue #185 decided to pay since
  WebRTC itself never required the exclusion.

  Receiving remote video (issue #91) mirrors the sending
  side, also split camera/screen (issue #185):
  PeerObserver::OnTrack() -> handleRemoteTrack() tells the
  two apart by the incoming track's id
  (kCameraTrackId/kScreenShareTrackId — the same strings
  given to CreateVideoTrack() on the sending side, carried
  over to the receiver via the SDP msid) and attaches a
  RemoteVideoSink (webrtc::VideoSinkInterface) to the
  matching PeerConnectionEntry::remoteCameraVideoSink/
  remoteScreenShareVideoSink slot, each guarding its own
  track against being attached twice independently of the
  other. RemoteVideoSink knows which kind it is and passes
  that along with every frame — remoteVideoFrameReceived()
  gained an isScreenShare parameter so the UI can show a
  participant's camera and screen share as two separate
  tiles instead of one clobbering the other. Frame
  conversion (I420 -> QImage via libyuv::I420ToARGB, the
  reverse of CallVideoTrackSource's ARGBToI420) and the hop
  to the GUI thread via QMetaObject::invokeMethod (OnFrame()
  fires on a WebRTC decode thread) are unchanged.
end note

note right of CallManager
  SFU (issue #123/#230/#231/#232): mesh's "one PeerConnection
  per remote participant" is replaced by "one publish
  PeerConnection (own audio/camera/screen) + one subscribe
  PeerConnection per remote feed", talking to Janus videoroom
  through chat-service's WebSocket proxy (janus_attach/
  janus_message/janus_event) instead of directly — mesh's own
  call_join/call_leave signaling still runs (chat-service uses
  it to assign the SFU room via sfuRoomAssigned()), but
  onCallRosterReceived() no longer drives ensurePeerConnection()/
  negotiateLocal() for it; mesh code stays in the file, unused,
  until #233 removes it.

  Both publish and subscribe connections skip ICE trickle
  entirely (the proxy has no "janus":"trickle" request) —
  PeerObserver::OnIceGatheringChange() waits for full candidate
  gathering, then handleIceGatheringComplete() sends the offer/
  answer (with every candidate embedded in the SDP) as Janus's
  "configure"/"start" request. handleLocalDescriptionSet()
  recognizes this by PeerConnectionEntry::janusHandle >= 0 and
  skips its usual immediate call_signal send for those entries.

  Publisher discovery: on our own "joined" event, and on every
  later room "event" carrying a publishers list, this class
  calls ensureSubscribeConnection(feedId, display) for each —
  "display" is the remote peer's login, set via setLocalLogin()
  when *they* joined as publisher, since Janus itself has no
  notion of chat-service logins. peers_ is reused verbatim for
  these subscribe connections (one per peerLogin, same as mesh
  used it) so handleRemoteTrack()/RemoteVideoSink above need no
  changes — only which PeerConnection the frames arrive from.

  Known gap: a track added to an already-connected
  publishConnection_ (enableVideo()/enableScreenShare() called
  mid-call) isn't pushed to already-subscribed peers — Janus
  doesn't do that automatically, and re-subscribing an existing
  subscribe handle to a newly added stream isn't implemented yet.
end note

